WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:07.680
I'm Jim Kloet.

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:10.830
I manage a team of data
scientists in Chicago,

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:14.130
and I am here to talk
about garbage data.

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:16.200
But before I talk
about garbage data,

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:17.897
I want to lead you
off with a quote.

00:00:17.897 --> 00:00:19.980
A lot of you have probably
seen this quote before.

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:22.030
It's about statistical models.

00:00:22.030 --> 00:00:24.990
That's from the famous
statistician George Box.

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:27.620
And he wrote a bunch of times,
at least the first part.

00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:31.080
All models are wrong,
but some are useful.

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:33.450
But then sometimes he'd go
on and he'd add things like,

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:37.450
so the question you need to ask
yourself is not is the model true?

00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:40.320
Because it never is, but
is the model good enough

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:42.450
for this particular application?

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:44.080
And I love this quote.

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:47.820
It's true. It's not
explicitly about garbage data,

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:50.370
but it turns out that you
can just cross out models

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:52.140
and replace it with data,

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:56.310
and the quote is still true
because all data are wrong,

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:57.850
but some are useful, right?

00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:00.570
There's no such thing as a
perfect data collection tool

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:02.435
or data retention tool.

00:01:02.435 --> 00:01:03.810
And so the question
that you need

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:06.700
to ask yourself isn't,
are the data true?

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:09.330
Because they're not, but
are the data good enough

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:10.930
for this particular application?

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:15.480
Right? And so in this
sentence that I'm thinking of

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:17.820
is us data professionals.

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:20.400
And depending on
where you're at,

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.770
whether you're an
industry or academia,

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:24.300
the titles are going to differ.

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:27.120
It could be a data scientist
or engineer or architect

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:29.730
or academic researcher.

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:32.460
The point is, though,
that we data professionals

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:35.550
are often the primary
deciders of data usefulness

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:37.060
in our organizations.

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:38.970
And a typical workflow,
at least for me,

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:40.590
kind of looks like this.

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:43.870
So we'll have some stakeholders
that are super enthusiastic.

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:46.230
I use a lot of emoji, so
if you don't like emoji,

00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:48.210
you should leave.

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:51.240
So stakeholders will
be really enthusiastic.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:52.740
They'll come to us
and ask for help,

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:54.100
like they brought us some data.

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:55.640
And I don't work in agriculture.

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:59.130
This is a metaphorical
Haystack, right?

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:00.940
And so then we
spring into action.

00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:02.080
And we think, OK, cool.

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:06.280
Where on this hypothetical
continuum of data usefulness

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:08.850
do we think that
these data should fall?

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:10.780
And if they're maximally useful,

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:14.040
right? The best case scenario
is that they are just

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:16.442
chock full of
insights, which you

00:02:16.442 --> 00:02:17.650
see the metaphor keeps going.

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:20.220
Now we've got needles,
answers to our questions,

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:23.700
solutions to our problems,
whatever the stakeholders are

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:24.400
looking for.

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:27.270
Because, I mean,
ultimately, stakeholders

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:29.650
expect that there's going to
be something in data, right?

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:32.160
Like that's the
promise of big data.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:35.220
And so even if we
make this assessment

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:37.770
that data are minimally
useful, the expectation

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:41.760
from our stakeholders is still
like there's non-zero insights

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:42.460
in there, right?

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:43.918
Like that there's
at least a needle

00:02:43.918 --> 00:02:46.840
in the haystack or two needles.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:49.390
And again, people are led to
believe that data are always

00:02:49.390 --> 00:02:50.720
going to be the solution here.

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:53.510
They're not going
to be the problem.

00:02:53.510 --> 00:02:56.000
But I think Meghan's
talk highlighted,

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:59.840
Max Kuhn's keynote this
morning also called out

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:02.420
data aren't always useful.
And in a lot of cases

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:05.930
data are just garbage.
And garbage data are

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:07.310
at all of our organizations.

00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:08.590
I think if you've

00:03:08.590 --> 00:03:10.120
worked with real world data,

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:12.350
you definitely have
encountered garbage data.

00:03:12.350 --> 00:03:14.860
And so you know,
then, that garbage data

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:17.830
is problematic and has costs
and can lead to real problems

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:19.070
within our organization.

00:03:19.070 --> 00:03:20.855
So my talk here, I
want to make sure

00:03:20.855 --> 00:03:22.730
you all walk away with
at least three points.

00:03:22.730 --> 00:03:25.090
First, I want to make sure
you can identify garbage

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:26.920
data in your organizations.

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:29.500
Second, I want to make
sure that you're aware of

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:31.975
and can identify the
costs of garbage data.

00:03:31.975 --> 00:03:34.600
And then finally, I want to make
sure that you're taking action

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:37.510
when you see garbage data.

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:38.010
All right.

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:41.240
So in the first point here
on identifying garbage data,

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:43.850
so we think an
operational definition

00:03:43.850 --> 00:03:45.060
is a good starting point.

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:47.900
And garbage data, I
think simply are just

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:50.400
data that aren't useful for
a particular application.

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:52.400
So if we have
stakeholders coming up

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:55.340
to us, then the first question
we really need to ask them is,

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:58.490
are the data that you're giving
us that we're working with

00:03:58.490 --> 00:04:02.380
relevant for the questions that
you're interested in asking?

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:05.140
And so say our
stakeholders come up

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:07.960
with questions about
hot dogs and then they

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:09.910
give us data like this.

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:13.510
And it's adorable and
it's possibly useful

00:04:13.510 --> 00:04:17.222
based on just the simple
dogs being in their-- hat tip

00:04:17.222 --> 00:04:19.430
to Hadley for this joke, by
the way, in the feedback.

00:04:19.430 --> 00:04:20.890
Thank you, Hadley.

00:04:20.890 --> 00:04:23.800
But as we're making our
decision about how the data are

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:26.080
useful or not, it's quite
likely that we'll probably

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:27.760
say, no, these data
aren't relevant

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:29.270
to the particular application.

00:04:29.270 --> 00:04:31.940
They're likely not going to be
able to answer your questions.

00:04:31.940 --> 00:04:35.090
But our questions usually
don't stop there, right?

00:04:35.090 --> 00:04:36.550
We've identified
the garbage data,

00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:39.410
but garbage data
aren't always garbage.

00:04:39.410 --> 00:04:41.600
They could be relevant
for a different thing.

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:45.100
So the next question we want
to ask our stakeholders,

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:46.900
is there another
application where

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:48.730
these data could be relevant?

00:04:48.730 --> 00:04:51.550
And so if your stakeholders
are anything like mine,

00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:52.790
they have lots of questions.

00:04:52.790 --> 00:04:54.250
It's not just that
their questions

00:04:54.250 --> 00:04:56.780
are about hot dogs or
one thing in particular.

00:04:56.780 --> 00:04:59.470
Oftentimes the data that
they have are probably

00:04:59.470 --> 00:05:02.030
relevant to something else
that they're interested in.

00:05:02.030 --> 00:05:04.120
And so working with
them to find out

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:06.640
if there's another question
that the data can be helpful

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:09.790
in answering seems like
is another reasonable step

00:05:09.790 --> 00:05:11.170
to take.

00:05:11.170 --> 00:05:12.970
I think that there's
one other step

00:05:12.970 --> 00:05:14.630
to take after this, though.

00:05:14.630 --> 00:05:17.170
Say you say no and
the data are not

00:05:17.170 --> 00:05:21.040
necessarily useful for
the secondary application.

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:22.750
I think it's safe
or fair to ask,

00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:26.140
are there reasons to expect that
these data are total garbage?

00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:28.490
Because, again, we've
worked with real world data.

00:05:28.490 --> 00:05:29.865
We know that there
are definitely

00:05:29.865 --> 00:05:33.880
cases where your data are
not useful for anything,

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:36.670
and that often requires
us to put on our sleuthing

00:05:36.670 --> 00:05:39.730
hats, which is usually a part
of any data professional's

00:05:39.730 --> 00:05:41.170
arsenal.

00:05:41.170 --> 00:05:43.240
I'm not going to go
into all of the ways

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:44.615
that data can be
totally garbage,

00:05:44.615 --> 00:05:46.823
but I'm going to walk through
at least a few examples

00:05:46.823 --> 00:05:49.280
because I'm sure that they
will resonate with some of us.

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:51.490
Meghan also had a ton of
awesome examples in her talk

00:05:51.490 --> 00:05:54.910
as well, so check out
that repo. So things can go

00:05:54.910 --> 00:05:56.210
wrong during data collection.

00:05:56.210 --> 00:05:57.890
I used to work with
handwriting data.

00:05:57.890 --> 00:06:00.310
Sometimes that
data is illegible.

00:06:00.310 --> 00:06:02.417
This is not data from any
of my previous studies.

00:06:02.417 --> 00:06:04.000
This is actually
from a New York Times

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:06.160
article about kids handwriting.

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:08.020
But the point I think,
is still well-taken

00:06:08.020 --> 00:06:10.540
that sometimes the
data is just completely

00:06:10.540 --> 00:06:13.720
borked from the
point of collection.

00:06:13.720 --> 00:06:15.620
It can also get screwed
up in retention.

00:06:15.620 --> 00:06:16.120
Right?

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:19.390
I've had a weird case
where some of my data

00:06:19.390 --> 00:06:22.190
were corrupted from a hard drive
failure, but not all of it.

00:06:22.190 --> 00:06:24.288
And it didn't become
clear until I'm

00:06:24.288 --> 00:06:26.080
doing a little bit more
digging and reading

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:28.705
a whole bunch of logs, which is
not my favorite way of spending

00:06:28.705 --> 00:06:29.560
time.

00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:31.810
And so retention is
another opportunity

00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:34.180
for things to really go wrong
and make your data totally

00:06:34.180 --> 00:06:36.700
garbage.

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:37.850
'90s kids.

00:06:37.850 --> 00:06:38.860
Steve Urkel.

00:06:38.860 --> 00:06:41.740
So a lot of times
the total garbage

00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:44.260
results from someone
accidentally borking the data.

00:06:44.260 --> 00:06:46.750
I have done this more
times than I can count,

00:06:46.750 --> 00:06:49.420
and if there's anyone in the
room who has not accidentally

00:06:49.420 --> 00:06:51.670
borked their data to make
it useless or very close

00:06:51.670 --> 00:06:54.328
to useless, raise your hand.

00:06:54.328 --> 00:06:55.870
Well, actually,
don't raise your hand

00:06:55.870 --> 00:06:57.745
because it's going to
happen sooner or later.

00:06:57.745 --> 00:06:59.540
So just be prepared
for that sort of thing.

00:06:59.540 --> 00:07:02.440
So people make mistakes
all of the time in data

00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:05.050
collection and retention,
and so that's sometimes

00:07:05.050 --> 00:07:06.980
how data will become
totally useless.

00:07:06.980 --> 00:07:08.440
I think a recent
case is where we

00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:12.110
had a lot of data that were
replaced by default values.

00:07:12.110 --> 00:07:14.530
And so when it looked
like 99% of our sample

00:07:14.530 --> 00:07:16.750
were default values,
we had to start

00:07:16.750 --> 00:07:20.110
wondering if that was going
to be useful for inference.

00:07:20.110 --> 00:07:22.540
The last case,
which I think makes

00:07:22.540 --> 00:07:24.940
a lot of news and headlines,
is that sometimes people

00:07:24.940 --> 00:07:26.998
intentionally bork their data.

00:07:26.998 --> 00:07:28.540
This is what I think
we used to think

00:07:28.540 --> 00:07:31.300
cybercriminals looked like
maybe this is what they actually

00:07:31.300 --> 00:07:31.880
look like.

00:07:31.880 --> 00:07:34.540
I'm not sure.

00:07:34.540 --> 00:07:38.050
I don't necessarily think
that this is the most common

00:07:38.050 --> 00:07:39.050
scenario, right?

00:07:39.050 --> 00:07:42.460
People usually seem to be
honest in my experience

00:07:42.460 --> 00:07:45.580
with their data, unless they're
really incentivized to have

00:07:45.580 --> 00:07:47.390
data look a very specific way.

00:07:47.390 --> 00:07:50.110
So I guess if those
cases are arising,

00:07:50.110 --> 00:07:53.110
which I don't think I've
ever had in my career, that

00:07:53.110 --> 00:07:55.570
might be an opportunity where
totally garbage data could

00:07:55.570 --> 00:07:56.770
be arising.

00:07:56.770 --> 00:07:59.740
So in those cases,
what do you do?

00:07:59.740 --> 00:08:02.240
You've identified that the
data are totally garbage.

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:03.560
Those need to be destroyed.

00:08:03.560 --> 00:08:06.517
I think in most cases. I can
point to a number of times

00:08:06.517 --> 00:08:09.100
when I've worked with folks that
have tried to make inferences

00:08:09.100 --> 00:08:10.900
from totally garbage
data, and we'll

00:08:10.900 --> 00:08:13.400
get into the consequences
of those in a little while.

00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:16.840
But if you can't put up
or if you can't totally

00:08:16.840 --> 00:08:18.760
destroy the data, put
up as much warning tape

00:08:18.760 --> 00:08:21.190
as you possibly can because
you want to make sure

00:08:21.190 --> 00:08:24.340
that nobody else is trying to
accidentally make inferences

00:08:24.340 --> 00:08:25.540
out of this.

00:08:25.540 --> 00:08:28.570
So identifying data is useful,
identifying garbage data

00:08:28.570 --> 00:08:29.210
is useful.

00:08:29.210 --> 00:08:31.880
But I called out that
there's a reason to do it.

00:08:31.880 --> 00:08:34.030
And it's because garbage data.

00:08:34.030 --> 00:08:36.760
Oops excuse me, I
had a summary slide.

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:41.620
So summing up part one, some
garbage data use not useful.

00:08:41.620 --> 00:08:44.030
Some garbage data are
useful for other stuff.

00:08:44.030 --> 00:08:46.280
Some not useful for anything.

00:08:46.280 --> 00:08:48.890
So why do we care?

00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:50.990
Well, it's because
garbage data have costs.

00:08:50.990 --> 00:08:54.100
And I think of those costs in
a couple of different ways.

00:08:54.100 --> 00:08:56.427
I think, first, of
these unavoidable costs

00:08:56.427 --> 00:08:58.510
that you're going to have
any time you have a data

00:08:58.510 --> 00:08:59.700
project, right.

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:03.688
This is just example cloud
bill, because every time

00:09:03.688 --> 00:09:05.230
you have a data
project, you're going

00:09:05.230 --> 00:09:07.630
to incur costs with
data collection,

00:09:07.630 --> 00:09:12.010
data retention, any kind of
support around those data.

00:09:12.010 --> 00:09:13.780
There's always
going to be a cost.

00:09:13.780 --> 00:09:16.030
Your data, like your cloud
provider doesn't know,

00:09:16.030 --> 00:09:19.150
probably doesn't care if
your data are garbage,

00:09:19.150 --> 00:09:21.670
those costs are going to happen,
whether your data are good

00:09:21.670 --> 00:09:23.350
or not.

00:09:23.350 --> 00:09:25.630
You might not be able
to avoid some costs,

00:09:25.630 --> 00:09:27.093
but the avoidable
costs, I think,

00:09:27.093 --> 00:09:28.510
are really the
ones you have to be

00:09:28.510 --> 00:09:30.970
on the lookout for
because that can really

00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:32.950
torpedo your organization.

00:09:32.950 --> 00:09:37.010
So first, the excessive
regular old data costs, right?

00:09:37.010 --> 00:09:39.935
If you don't catch the fact
that you have garbage data,

00:09:39.935 --> 00:09:42.310
well, you're just going to
keep incurring bills and bills

00:09:42.310 --> 00:09:43.790
and bills and they add up.

00:09:43.790 --> 00:09:46.030
And I suspect that in a
lot of organizations that

00:09:46.030 --> 00:09:48.080
are bigger than $7,400 a month.

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:52.060
So if you can catch garbage
data when it happens,

00:09:52.060 --> 00:09:54.790
that can save a lot of costs.

00:09:54.790 --> 00:09:57.910
Opportunity costs, I think, are
another really important one

00:09:57.910 --> 00:10:00.160
to call out that are
probably a lot less

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:02.918
easy to point to because
they're not tangible.

00:10:02.918 --> 00:10:03.710
They didn't happen.

00:10:03.710 --> 00:10:05.450
You can't say I missed this thing.

00:10:05.450 --> 00:10:09.580
Right? But philosophically,
garbage data necessarily

00:10:09.580 --> 00:10:11.210
doesn't have any value.

00:10:11.210 --> 00:10:13.570
So any time you spend
looking into it,

00:10:13.570 --> 00:10:16.840
is time you should have been
spending doing something else.

00:10:16.840 --> 00:10:18.140
But we're practical.

00:10:18.140 --> 00:10:19.520
We're not here for philosophy.

00:10:19.520 --> 00:10:23.000
And so like from a
practical perspective,

00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:25.000
you see that there's a
lot of interesting things

00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:27.458
that you could be working on
as a data professional instead

00:10:27.458 --> 00:10:29.440
of plumbing the depths
of these garbage data

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:32.267
and trying to validate that
your stakeholders might

00:10:32.267 --> 00:10:33.850
be able to find
something or might not

00:10:33.850 --> 00:10:34.940
be able to find something.

00:10:34.940 --> 00:10:37.450
And I think that leads
to another level of cost,

00:10:37.450 --> 00:10:40.090
which are these people costs.

00:10:40.090 --> 00:10:41.650
I like to learn things.

00:10:41.650 --> 00:10:45.130
I like to help other people
learn things and answer

00:10:45.130 --> 00:10:46.040
their questions.

00:10:46.040 --> 00:10:48.560
And you can't do that
with garbage data.

00:10:48.560 --> 00:10:50.890
And eventually, after having
to work with garbage data

00:10:50.890 --> 00:10:53.380
over and over and over
again, data professionals

00:10:53.380 --> 00:10:55.780
are probably going to go find
something else to do or go

00:10:55.780 --> 00:10:58.930
look for data that may
or may not be garbage.

00:10:58.930 --> 00:11:00.410
I guess, as a spoiler,

00:11:00.410 --> 00:11:02.110
the grass isn't
greener everywhere.

00:11:02.110 --> 00:11:04.780
If anyone's worried about their
organizations being especially

00:11:04.780 --> 00:11:05.150
borked.

00:11:05.150 --> 00:11:06.080
It's dirty everywhere.

00:11:06.080 --> 00:11:10.510
But people will continue to
look if the data that they're

00:11:10.510 --> 00:11:13.450
working with isn't allowing
them to learn things.

00:11:13.450 --> 00:11:16.930
And I think that leads to
maybe the most obvious kinds

00:11:16.930 --> 00:11:18.830
of costs, which are
decision costs, right?

00:11:18.830 --> 00:11:22.540
Because if you're making
decisions with garbage data,

00:11:22.540 --> 00:11:25.520
you don't know what's
going to happen. Right?

00:11:25.520 --> 00:11:27.340
Like you're making
decisions basically

00:11:27.340 --> 00:11:30.250
with random noise
in a lot of cases.

00:11:30.250 --> 00:11:33.160
And that means something
good could happen,

00:11:33.160 --> 00:11:36.190
but not because of any
smart things we did.

00:11:36.190 --> 00:11:38.900
But lots of bad things
could happen too.

00:11:38.900 --> 00:11:40.570
And that's why I
think that ultimately

00:11:40.570 --> 00:11:44.240
these avoidable costs can
really be catastrophic.

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:48.190
Right? So if you think about
making bad decisions after all

00:11:48.190 --> 00:11:49.690
of your data
professionals have left

00:11:49.690 --> 00:11:52.120
looking for greener
pastures after having spent

00:11:52.120 --> 00:11:55.120
all of your money and retaining
a bunch of garbage data, that

00:11:55.120 --> 00:11:58.760
feels like a recipe for
disaster for an organization.

00:11:58.760 --> 00:12:02.117
And so the costs can really,
really, really add up.

00:12:02.117 --> 00:12:03.700
Garbage data is
something that we need

00:12:03.700 --> 00:12:05.870
to care a great deal about.

00:12:05.870 --> 00:12:08.160
So now I'm remembering
my summary slide.

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:11.720
So in summation, part two,
think about garbage data costs

00:12:11.720 --> 00:12:14.180
in a couple of different
ways unavoidable costs.

00:12:14.180 --> 00:12:16.680
You're going to have those any
time you have a data project.

00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:20.410
Avoidable costs, though,
can be catastrophic.

00:12:20.410 --> 00:12:22.970
So finally, we
should take action

00:12:22.970 --> 00:12:24.563
when we see garbage data.

00:12:24.563 --> 00:12:25.730
What does that mean, though?

00:12:25.730 --> 00:12:28.690
So we're the primary
deciders of data usefulness.

00:12:28.690 --> 00:12:30.550
The first thing I
think it means is

00:12:30.550 --> 00:12:32.410
when people come to
us with questions

00:12:32.410 --> 00:12:35.500
and bring us some data, we
have to be honest with them

00:12:35.500 --> 00:12:37.120
and direct with them.

00:12:37.120 --> 00:12:39.940
I know that I don't like
to give people bad news.

00:12:39.940 --> 00:12:41.660
At various points in my life

00:12:41.660 --> 00:12:45.050
People have called me Jim the
dream crusher, and I felt bad.

00:12:45.050 --> 00:12:47.890
It was a joke, but
I felt bad about it

00:12:47.890 --> 00:12:49.790
and I don't want to
be in that position.

00:12:49.790 --> 00:12:53.050
But the consequences of
not being honest and direct

00:12:53.050 --> 00:12:54.610
with your stakeholders
and letting

00:12:54.610 --> 00:12:56.110
them know when
garbage data happens

00:12:56.110 --> 00:12:58.690
are a lot worse than the
consequences of having some bummed

00:12:58.690 --> 00:13:00.110
stakeholders right now.

00:13:00.110 --> 00:13:02.050
We don't want to bum
any stakeholders out,

00:13:02.050 --> 00:13:04.870
but again, I think that we are
obligated as the people who

00:13:04.870 --> 00:13:07.240
are kind of the line of
defense against garbage data

00:13:07.240 --> 00:13:09.680
to say this is garbage.

00:13:09.680 --> 00:13:10.280
My five.

00:13:10.280 --> 00:13:12.360
OK, cool.

00:13:12.360 --> 00:13:15.750
So the second thing, right, we
don't want to bum people out.

00:13:15.750 --> 00:13:17.530
I think we need to
lead with empathy.

00:13:17.530 --> 00:13:19.795
And this probably
is just kind of par

00:13:19.795 --> 00:13:21.420
for the course for
most of us, but it's

00:13:21.420 --> 00:13:23.610
useful to say it explicitly.

00:13:23.610 --> 00:13:26.920
Our stakeholders have a lot
of things that they need.

00:13:26.920 --> 00:13:29.190
Our stakeholders
have demands on them,

00:13:29.190 --> 00:13:30.870
they have pressures on them.

00:13:30.870 --> 00:13:33.570
They're not using garbage data
or asking us to play around

00:13:33.570 --> 00:13:35.220
with garbage data
because they think

00:13:35.220 --> 00:13:36.940
that it's a good way for
us to spend their time.

00:13:36.940 --> 00:13:39.398
They're asking us because they
need our help for something.

00:13:39.398 --> 00:13:41.430
And so if we're not
leading with empathy,

00:13:41.430 --> 00:13:43.080
we end up with
salty stakeholders

00:13:43.080 --> 00:13:44.830
who don't want to
work with us anymore.

00:13:44.830 --> 00:13:47.010
And working with
salty stakeholders

00:13:47.010 --> 00:13:49.190
is a good way to not
have a job anymore,

00:13:49.190 --> 00:13:53.220
or be a professional, a data
professional anymore.

00:13:53.220 --> 00:13:55.680
Another useful
thing to try and do

00:13:55.680 --> 00:13:58.200
is if you can influence
your organization

00:13:58.200 --> 00:14:00.280
to treat all problems
as data problems

00:14:00.280 --> 00:14:02.370
from the very
beginning. All projects

00:14:02.370 --> 00:14:06.100
should be data projects until
otherwise kind of considered.

00:14:06.100 --> 00:14:08.950
So default to treating
projects as data projects.

00:14:08.950 --> 00:14:11.940
So an ideal workflow
is when my stakeholders

00:14:11.940 --> 00:14:15.180
come to me with questions rather
than coming to me with data

00:14:15.180 --> 00:14:17.940
that gives me the opportunity
and my team the opportunity

00:14:17.940 --> 00:14:20.130
to evaluate which data
sets might be most

00:14:20.130 --> 00:14:23.148
appropriate to help them answer
those questions, which makes it

00:14:23.148 --> 00:14:25.440
at least more likely that
we're going to find something

00:14:25.440 --> 00:14:26.700
in there.

00:14:26.700 --> 00:14:29.610
And again, like, they
may not be as happy

00:14:29.610 --> 00:14:31.560
as if you'd given them
a huge pile of needles,

00:14:31.560 --> 00:14:35.020
but they'll be grateful that you
didn't leave them empty handed.

00:14:35.020 --> 00:14:37.420
And then finally,
I think we have

00:14:37.420 --> 00:14:39.400
to be patient
because organizations

00:14:39.400 --> 00:14:40.700
are used to doing things

00:14:40.700 --> 00:14:44.740
however they've been doing them.
And coming in and saying, hey,

00:14:44.740 --> 00:14:48.650
you're doing it wrong is
never going to drive change.

00:14:48.650 --> 00:14:49.940
I have made that mistake.

00:14:49.940 --> 00:14:51.790
It's a bad idea.

00:14:51.790 --> 00:14:53.360
So be patient.

00:14:53.360 --> 00:14:56.250
Build those relationships and.

00:14:56.250 --> 00:14:57.430
If you do all of this stuff.

00:14:57.430 --> 00:14:58.687
Like if you're.

00:14:58.687 --> 00:15:01.270
I think the third part here, the
actions that we can take here

00:15:01.270 --> 00:15:03.550
is we have to be honest,
we have to be empathetic,

00:15:03.550 --> 00:15:06.070
make sure we're treating all
projects as data projects

00:15:06.070 --> 00:15:08.167
and making sure
that we're patient.

00:15:08.167 --> 00:15:10.000
I think that'll help
us accomplish all three

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:12.070
of these points here of
being able to identify

00:15:12.070 --> 00:15:16.120
garbage data, the costs, making
sure that we're taking action.

00:15:16.120 --> 00:15:18.400
And I guess to
wrap it up, I mean,

00:15:18.400 --> 00:15:21.130
if you do everything I suggest
your data are still going

00:15:21.130 --> 00:15:24.010
to be wrong no matter what.

00:15:24.010 --> 00:15:27.790
There is no changing that,
but there's a better chance

00:15:27.790 --> 00:15:29.540
that they could turn
out to be useful.

00:15:29.540 --> 00:15:31.090
And that's our goal.

00:15:31.090 --> 00:15:33.840
Thank you all very
much for your time.

